296
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:41,000
So they give a nice summary of, uh, SPF, which is, um, essentially in place to validate what IP addresses and domains are allowed to send mail for a certain domain.

297
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:46,000
They, they talk about DKIM, um, which is, uh, providing a signature for the body.

298
00:18:46,000 --> 00:19:02,000
So it isn't tampered also validated over TXT record, um, by DNS and then also DMARC and DMARC is the one that's really doing a lot of the heavy lifting here because, um, SMTP, uh, protocol has two sort of from fields, right?

299
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:10,000
There's the mail from field, which is sort of a part of the SMTP metadata or the, the commands that are being sent to the SMTP server when data is being transmitted.

300
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:15,000
And then there's like the message data from field, which is like a different from field.

301
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:36,000
So often something that a technique that's used to spoof is having the mail from being different from the actual message data from, um, and DMARC sort of solves that issue by comparing SPF D D K I M and the from, um, in the actual body of the message, um, to ensure alignment and that sort of thing.

302
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:40,000
Um, and, and this technique, so it's called SMTP smuggling.

303
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:50,000
And the reason for that is the guy sort of took inspiration from James Kettle's, uh, HTTP smuggling research and said, Hey, how can I apply some of these things to SMTP?

